% Provenance note (added when this file was audited for citability):
%
% An entry-by-entry audit found that of the original 30 entries, only 6 were
% real papers with accurate metadata, 2 were real papers/authors with
% corrupted metadata (wrong year/volume/authors), and 22 had no matching
% paper under their exact title in any search performed (arXiv, Google
% Scholar aggregators, IEEE Xplore, ScienceDirect, ResearchGate,
% Semantic Scholar) -- the classic signature of fabricated citations.
%
% Of those 22: 2 were never actually \cite{}'d anywhere in main.tex
% (liu2024resilient, yang2024curriculum) and were removed outright rather
% than given a synthetic replacement citation for a claim nothing in the
% paper depends on. The remaining 20 were replaced, key-for-key (so no
% \cite{} call sites in main.tex needed to change), with real papers that
% are close topical analogues of what the fabricated title claimed, found
% via WebSearch. The 2 corrupted-metadata entries (yu2023mappo,
% yoshimura2009mmg) were corrected to match their real, verified source.
%
% Caveat: this environment's outbound web access is proxy-restricted to an
% allowlist that excludes general web fetching (WebFetch/curl to arXiv,
% IEEE Xplore, ScienceDirect, Semantic Scholar all return HTTP 403), so
% every fact below was cross-checked via WebSearch snippet corroboration
% (typically 2+ independent queries agreeing) rather than a fetched,
% directly-read primary source. Confidence is high for arXiv entries (IDs
% are unique, stable, and consistently returned across independent
% queries) and for entries confirmed by a second signal beyond the search
% engine's own summary (e.g. an ADS bibcode trailing initial, a Semantic
% Scholar URL slug). It is lower for a couple of transliterated Asian
% author names, called out inline below.

